WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
A learning organization&nbsp;
encourages personal mastery

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.220
and cultivates open feedback

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.580
to see problems and opportunities on all levels.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.920
Some argue that learning organizations

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.930
attract and retain more talents.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:16.110
Others say that there is a&nbsp;
competitive advantage for

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.540
an organization whose people learn faster

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.250
than the people of other organizations.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.550
Here are 6 characteristics most have in common.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.750
They Cherish: An Open Culture.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.380
Learning organizations encourage&nbsp;
everyone to share information,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.260
admit to mistakes and practice

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.690
giving and taking constructive criticism.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:38.580
Once the problem is found,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.610
they try to understand its root cause and fix it.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.610
To achieve such a culture: walls are removed,

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.120
information is shared and&nbsp;
leaders show their human sides.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.890
They Design and Implement: Feedback Loops

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.340
Some establish 360 degrees surveys,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.940
in which people assess themselves,&nbsp;
their peers and their bosses.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.530
Employees at 5-star hotels&nbsp;
ask guests for their opinions.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.020
Top schools may videotape teachers

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.310
so they can later study themselves.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.370
Some even make feedback a team effort.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:15.840
Before any new project, they all&nbsp;
get together to kick things off.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.720
After the project they meet again to share

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.980
and reflect on what has happened.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.390
They Promote: Personal Mastery

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:28.860
Employees try to achieve&nbsp;
personal mastery in their fields.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.540
Once they become experts,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.070
they feel proud of their work,

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.660
they are motivated intrinsically

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.960
and they can create positive&nbsp;
change wherever they are.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.700
For example, a cleaner might come&nbsp;
up with an idea on how to save water

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.340
and an accountant on how to save bank fees.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.720
The job of the boss is to connect&nbsp;
all experts and give directions.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:53.740
They Plan for: Intelligent Fast Failure.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.870
When they build something new,

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.660
they don’t spend time to&nbsp;
make assumptions on paper.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.380
Instead, they create what’s&nbsp;
called Minimum Viable Product,

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.380
a simple prototype with only the core functions.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.320
This is then presented to&nbsp;
users as early as possible

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.880
to test what they think.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.530
Because it is imperfect,

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.960
even friends give their honest opinions.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:18.190
The goal: fail fast, but&nbsp;
collect intelligent information

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.320
so you can improve while going forward.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.130
They Steal: Best Practices

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.590
Picasso apparently said that

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.200
"good artists borrow, great artists steal".

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.230
Learning organizations study others,

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.350
steal best practices and then implement them fast.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:39.280
The newspaper The Economists&nbsp;
took advice from George Orwell;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.350
its editors never use jargon

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:42.790
if everyday English works.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:46.240
Printing manufacturers stole the razor-and-blades

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.650
business model from Gillette,

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.650
selling printers cheap but ink expensive.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:54.640
They Cultivate: A Common Vision

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.260
A learning organization prospers when&nbsp;
all members share a common vision.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.900
That way employees can understand

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:02.980
the importance of their role,

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.160
connect the dots and develop systems thinking.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.720
When goals are clear,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.610
regulations can be reduced

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:13.000
and people can create their own&nbsp;
personal benchmarks of success.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.380
This reduces bureaucracy,&nbsp;
authority and corruption.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.910
Salesmen and author Zig Ziglar once wrote:

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:25.330
"the only thing worse than training&nbsp;
people and having them leave,"

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.790
"is not training them and having them stay."

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:29.830
At a learning organization

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:32.710
education happens as a&nbsp;
side-product of working together,

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:35.080
as everybody learns from each other

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.420
to adapt to whatever the future might bring.

